     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What does a commercial HVAC installation project in Chicago actually include?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A commercial install runs as a project, not an equipment drop. Work starts with a load review of the space. Next comes equipment choice and a zone layout. Then the unit is set or rigged into place. Gas, power, controls, startup and handover follow. City & Suburban installs American Standard equipment and repairs all makes and models. So the plan is built around the building, not one catalog. Swapping a system that is already in place is a different scope. That work sits on the commercial HVAC replacement page."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How does City & Suburban approach a commercial install differently from a house?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Scale, access and downtime all change. City & Suburban has been family owned since 1952. Rob runs it now, second generation of the founding family. So an owner sits in the estimate talk. NATE-certified technicians handle startup and controls. The price is put in writing before work starts, with no hidden fees. Good scheduling works around store hours, roof access and the other trades on site. Across the full commercial HVAC range, the score is 4.98 from 204 Google reviews. Yelp shows 4.9 from 251 reviews, and the BBB grade is A+."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Rooftop units or split systems: which suits a Chicago commercial building?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "The building decides, not taste. Single-story shops, restaurants and warehouses often take packaged rooftop units. The roof has to carry the weight. That choice keeps the equipment out of the leased area and saves floor space. Suites inside multi-tenant buildings often have no roof rights. Those spaces usually take split systems. An indoor air handler pairs with an outdoor unit. Mixed layouts are common in Chicago. City & Suburban installs American Standard equipment and repairs all makes and models. Where the ducts cannot carry the new airflow, commercial ductwork joins the plan."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why does a load calculation come before any equipment is ordered?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Loads swing with people, lights, kitchen gear, glass area and fresh-air needs. A tonnage guess from floor area is often wrong. Too big is as bad as too small. Oversized units short cycle, leave the air damp and wear parts early. Undersized units run flat out and still lose ground on a hot day. A sound proposal starts with the space and its planned use, and checks duct capacity at the same time. Commercial ductwork that cannot carry the new airflow becomes part of the plan, not a surprise after startup."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How is zoning planned for a space that heats and cools unevenly?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Uneven is the normal case. A south-facing storefront and an inside meeting room rarely want the same airflow. One thermostat in the wrong spot makes every other area wrong. Zoning splits a space into areas with separate control. That can mean more than one unit, dampers on one system, or separate indoor heads. Zones work best when they follow how the space is used through the day, with the controls set to match. Settling zones before the install costs far less than adding them later. The commercial HVAC range covers both."
